.lua
book.chapter(37, "DSLtk.hpp: trees, visitors, and rewriting")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Tagged trees provide a reusable vocabulary"
.pp
ASTNode is the toolkit's runtime tree representation. Leaf and node helpers
use compile-time tag strings while storing runtime payloads and children.
Dump produces a readable structural representation useful in tests and
diagnostics. This generic AST is distinct from both MetaML's internal
language-specific expression objects and a C++ expression-template DSL.
Select the representation whose invariants match the pass you are writing.
.lua
book.code("cpp", [=[#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <cassert>
int main() {
    auto tree = dsl::node<"add">(
        dsl::leaf<"num">(2), dsl::leaf<"num">(3));
    assert(tree.dump() == "(add (num 2) (num 3))");
    auto schema = dsl::ast_schema<"add">(dsl::ast_schema<"num">());
    int total = 0;
    auto visitor = dsl::compile_visitor(schema,
        dsl::on<"add">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}),
        dsl::on<"num">([&](const dsl::ASTNode& n) {
            total += std::stoi(n.value());
        }));
    visitor.walk(tree);
    assert(total == 5);
}]=], "A complete schema-checked native visitor")
.endlua
.pp
The example counts numeric leaf payloads; it is not a general addition
interpreter. In particular, accepting an add tag does not establish binary
arity or numeric typing of every descendant. A semantic pass must validate
those properties separately. This distinction prevents a convenient generic
visitor mechanism from being mistaken for a full type system for the
language represented by the tree.
.sh 2 "Schemas check tag coverage"
.pp
Derive_visitor can discover accepted tags from a runtime schema tree.
Compile_visitor can check a static vocabulary during host compilation using
ast_schema. Every schema tag needs a handler. A visited tag outside the
schema is rejected. The schema establishes tag coverage, not exact child
counts or payload constraints; its example values do not define a complete
grammar. State this scope clearly when exposing visitor builders to users.
.lua
book.code("d-fragment", [=[import std.conv : to;
enum schema = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(0));
int total;
auto visitor = compile_visitor!schema(
    on!"add"((ref ASTNode n) {}),
    on!"num"((ref ASTNode n) { total += to!int(n.value()); }));
auto tree = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(2), leaf!"num"(3));
visitor.walk(tree);
assert(total == 5);]=], "D validates a compile-time schema through CTFE")
.endlua
.pp
Handlers can receive a node alone or a compile-time ASTTag followed by the
node. The tag permits specialization while payloads remain runtime values.
This is template-generated dispatch, not runtime JIT compilation. D uses
its own compile-time evaluation and template facilities to express the
corresponding contract. The native mechanisms differ while the accepted
tree vocabulary and handler behavior can agree.
.sh 2 "Dispatch and traversal are separate operations"
.pp
Visit dispatches one node; walk traverses a tree in preorder or postorder.
Preorder sees the parent first, while postorder sees children first. Mutable
handlers can affect the structure being traversed, so choose an order and
mutation policy deliberately. If a callback inserts children, decide whether
those children should be visited now, on another pass, or not at all.
Unspecified expectations here produce difficult rewrite bugs.
.pp
Const C++ handlers are a useful default for analyses. They communicate that
a pass collects information without changing syntax. A separate transformation
can return a new tree when immutable-style reasoning is preferable. In D,
public visitor operations use ref ASTNode, so the implementation must retain
the intended read-only discipline when mutation is not part of the pass.
Host syntax alone does not establish semantic purity.
.sh 2 "Rewriting needs a strategy and a stopping rule"
.pp
The toolkit offers tagged AST rewrite rules and separate first-order term
rewriting facilities. A rule identifies a matching shape and constructs a
replacement. A set of rules is not automatically terminating or confluent.
Rule order, traversal order, and repeated application policy determine which
result is obtained when several rules match. Document those choices rather
than describing every rewrite collection as an optimizer that finds a
unique normal form.
.lua
book.algorithm("Review a tree rewrite", {
    "State the accepted node shape and payload constraints.",
    "Check whether replacement duplicates, erases, or reorders children.",
    "Establish that those changes preserve the source language's effects.",
    "Choose preorder or postorder according to required child information.",
    "Bound repeated application or prove a decreasing measure.",
    "Test semantic preservation and the intended structural change."
})
.endlua
.pp
For constant folding, use the source language's arithmetic rather than
accidentally inheriting unchecked native signed overflow. For algebraic
simplification, consider exceptional values and effects: replacing x times
zero with zero can erase the evaluation of x. An AST rewrite library
provides traversal and replacement machinery; the language designer remains
responsible for whether a proposed identity is valid in the chosen semantics.
.sh 2 "Annotations and persistence"
.pp
Source spans, inferred types, and binding identities often need to survive
transformation. Generic tags and text do not automatically encode them.
Choose an annotation strategy and define how a replacement receives source
provenance. Copying the parent's span can be useful for a folded literal,
while merging unrelated spans may require a richer diagnostic model.
Treat this as part of the pass contract rather than an afterthought.
.pp
Persistent trees and mutable trees have different lifetime and invalidation
rules. Native references into a child collection may become invalid after
mutation. A path of child indices can become stale after insertion even
when the underlying storage remains valid. Analyses retained across passes
should therefore use explicit versioning or stable identities rather than
assuming a pointer or index always denotes the same semantic object.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read ASTNode, ASTSchema, derive_visitor, compile_visitor, and rewrite facilities
in DSLtk.hpp with their D counterparts. The toolkit manual's visitor chapter
and examples/08-ast-creation.cpp through the rewrite examples provide native
entry points. Add a validator enforcing binary add nodes, then a separate
folding pass. Test an unknown tag, incorrect arity, and a valid nested tree,
explaining which layer rejects each malformed input.
